---
import GuidePage from "../components/GuidePage.astro";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "How do I calculate standard deviation?",
    a: "Find the variance (the average squared distance from the mean, dividing by n − 1 for a sample or N for a population), then take its square root.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is the difference between sample and population standard deviation?",
    a: "Population standard deviation (σ) divides the sum of squares by N and describes a full population. Sample standard deviation (s) divides by n − 1 and estimates the population's spread from a sample.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is standard deviation the square root of variance?",
    a: "Yes. s = √s² and σ = √σ². Standard deviation shares the units of your data, while variance is in squared units.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does the calculator upload my data?",
    a: "No. Everything runs in your browser; nothing you paste is sent to a server.",
  },
];
---

<GuidePage
  title="Standard Deviation Calculator — Sample & Population, with Steps"
  description="Calculate standard deviation online for a sample or a population. Paste your numbers to get standard deviation, variance, mean and sum of squares, with the working shown."
  path="/standard-deviation-calculator"
  heading="Standard Deviation Calculator"
  intro="Standard deviation is the square root of variance. Use the free calculator to get both for a sample or a population, with every step shown."
  breadcrumbName="Standard Deviation Calculator"
  faqs={faqs}
  published="2026-10-23"
  modified="2026-10-23"
>
  <h2>Calculate standard deviation now</h2>
  <p>
    Open the <a href="/">variance calculator</a>, paste your numbers (commas, spaces, or
    spreadsheet columns all work), and choose sample or population. The result panel shows
    the standard deviation alongside the variance, mean, count, and sum of squares.
  </p>

  <h2>Formulas</h2>
  <p>Sample standard deviation: <code>s = √( Σ(x − x̄)² / (n − 1) )</code></p>
  <p>Population standard deviation: <code>σ = √( Σ(x − μ)² / N )</code></p>

  <h2>Sample or population?</h2>
  <p>
    Choose <strong>population</strong> when your data includes every member of the group
    (for example, all test scores in one class). Choose <strong>sample</strong> when your
    data is a subset used to estimate a larger group. When unsure, sample is the safer choice.
  </p>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>
    For <code>2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9</code> the mean is 5 and the sum of squares is 32.
    Population standard deviation is √(32 / 8) = <strong>2</strong>; sample standard deviation
    is √(32 / 7) ≈ <strong>2.1381</strong>. The full method is in
    <a href="/how-to-calculate-variance">how to calculate variance</a>.
  </p>
</GuidePage>
